  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A data set has mean $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=6$ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=10.8.$ Find the $z$-score of an observation $x=10.5,$ giving your answer to $2$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "The $z$-score of an observation $x$ is defined by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{x-\\overset{\u2015}{x}}{\\sigma }.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations an observation is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\nHere, we're told that the mean is $\\overset{\u2015}{x}=6,$ and we can calculate the standard deviation as\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{10.8}.\n$$\n\nSo, the $z$-score of the observation $x=10.5$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{\u2015}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\  & \u2248\\frac{10.5-6}{\\sqrt{10.8}} \\\\  & \u22481.37\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.",
